## Descriptor-shaped iteration.
##
## A value of an `Iter`-implementing type `'c` is an immutable *descriptor*
## for a stream of elements of the associated type `'elem`. `next` advances
## the descriptor by one step, returning `Step { elem, next_it }` when an
## element is available, or `Done` at end-of-stream.
##
## The receiver `'c` is not mutated — `next` returns the *next descriptor*
## rather than modifying the current one. This lets iterators compose
## purely (no shared state, no ordering hazards) and lets the compile-time
## fusion pass recognize `xs.iter{}.filter(p).map(f).fold(init, g)` as the
## same shape as the range/seq-fused loop.
##
## For stateful / effectful iteration (generators, cursors, IO streams),
## see the parked `StatefulIter` (#445) — that ability sits on top of an
## effect row and drives via `perform`.
##
## Concrete impls forward to native fold/get loops; the ability method is
## rarely called directly. See `std::collections::iter` for the standard
## free-function chain: `iter::filter`, `iter::map`, `iter::fold`,
## `iter::sum`, `iter::for_each`.

## Step result: end-of-stream sentinel or (element, next-descriptor) pair.
##
## Kept as a distinct variant rather than `Option ({elem, next_it})` so the
## fusion peel can pattern-match on the ctor name directly and so future
## specializations (unboxed `elem`, tail-call resume) can extend the
## variant without churning `Option` at every user callsite.
pub type IterStep 'c 'elem = | IterDone, IterStep { elem: 'elem, next_it: 'c } |

ability Iter for 'c having 'elem where {
  ## Advance the descriptor. `IterDone` marks end-of-stream. `IterStep`
  ## exposes the current element and the successor descriptor.
  next: { it: 'c } -> IterStep 'c 'elem
}
